---
/**
 * Methodology and caveats, in the register of an analyst firm's appendix — and
 * completely honest, because the honesty is the actual content.
 *
 * The synonym lists and score formulas are imported from the modules that
 * implement them, so this page cannot describe rules the code does not follow.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { HEDGE_WORDS, NO_WORDS, YES_WORDS } from '../../runner/analyze.ts'
import { SCORE_DEFINITIONS } from '../lib/scores.ts'
import { DEFAULT_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS, DEFAULT_SAMPLES } from '../../runner/run.ts'
import { getModels, getQuestions } from '../lib/data.ts'
import { REPO_URL, routes, sourceUrl } from '../lib/urls.ts'

const questions = getQuestions()
const models = getModels()
const asOf = models[0]?.pricing.asOf ?? 'the date recorded in models.json'
---

<Base
  title="Methodology and caveats"
  description="How the Sandwich Classification Benchmark is conducted, what it measures, and what it does not."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Methodology and caveats</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      This appendix describes how the research is conducted and, more importantly, the limits of
      what it can support. Every figure in this publication is produced by the code linked from this
      page.
    </p>

    <h2 id="questions">1. Research questions</h2>

    <p>
      {questions.length} questions are evaluated each period. All follow a single template, which is enforced
      by the schema rather than by convention:
    </p>

    <ul>
      {
        questions.map((question) => (
          <li>
            <q>{question.text}</q>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <p>
      Every question ends with <q>One word answer.</q> That is not stylistic. It is what makes the instruction-compliance
      measure below mean anything: a compliance rate is only meaningful if compliance was actually requested.<sup
        ><a href="#fn1" id="fnref1">1</a></sup
      >
    </p>

    <h2 id="parameters">2. Model parameters</h2>

    <p>
      Each model receives the question as a single user message with no system prompt. Maximum
      output is capped at {DEFAULT_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS} tokens — enough for a model to demonstrate that
      it is ignoring the instruction, and bounded enough that doing so costs a known amount.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Temperature is not set.</strong> Pinning it to zero across several vendors would not make
      them comparable, because they implement it differently, and it would suppress exactly the week-to-week
      variation this research exists to observe.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sampling">3. Sampling and aggregation</h2>

    <p>
      Each model is asked each question {DEFAULT_SAMPLES} times per period. Language model output is not
      deterministic; a single sample records what happened once. Three is an odd number, so a majority
      verdict exists.
    </p>

    <p>
      Aggregates are <strong>medians, not means</strong>. With three samples, one cold start or one
      retried request drags a mean somewhere unrepresentative, and the report would then be
      describing an artefact of the collection rather than the model.
    </p>

    <p>
      Where a model's samples are evenly split between two verdicts, its position is recorded as
      non-committal. It has not given a consistent answer, and reporting either side as
      <q>its</q> position would be taking a side on its behalf.
    </p>

    <h2 id="latency">4. What latency measures</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>Total latency</strong> is wall-clock time from issuing the request to finishing reading
      the response. It includes DNS resolution, TLS negotiation, network transit, provider-side queueing,
      and generation.
      <strong>It is not a measure of inference speed</strong> and this publication does not present it
      as one.<sup><a href="#fn2" id="fnref2">2</a></sup>
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Time to first token</strong> is the interval before the first <em>content</em> token arrives.
      For models that reason before answering, this can be many seconds even when the eventual answer
      is a single word — the reasoning is not counted as content, deliberately, because counting it would
      report a near-zero interval for a model that had said nothing yet.
    </p>

    <p>
      Measurements are taken from a GitHub-hosted runner in a region GitHub selects and does not
      publish. A provider with a point of presence near that runner appears faster for reasons
      unrelated to its model. Open-weights models are served by a host, and the host's hardware
      dominates: the Llama figures measure Together AI's serving, not Meta's model.
    </p>

    <h2 id="verdicts">5. How answers are classified</h2>

    <p>
      An answer is normalised — trimmed, stripped of surrounding quotes, markdown emphasis and
      trailing punctuation, lowercased — and then classified. <strong
        >Only a single-word answer receives a yes or no verdict.</strong
      > Anything longer is recorded as non-committal, including <q>Yes, but…</q>, because a model
      that wrote a paragraph has told us something different from one that wrote <q>Yes</q>.
    </p>

    <div class="word-lists">
      <div>
        <h3>Counted as affirmative</h3>
        <ul class="word-list">
          {
            YES_WORDS.map((word) => (
              <li>
                <code>{word}</code>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Counted as negative</h3>
        <ul class="word-list">
          {
            NO_WORDS.map((word) => (
              <li>
                <code>{word}</code>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Recognised hedges</h3>
        <ul class="word-list">
          {
            HEDGE_WORDS.map((word) => (
              <li>
                <code>{word}</code>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="list-source">
      These lists are imported directly from
      <a href={sourceUrl('src/runner/analyze.ts')}><code>src/runner/analyze.ts</code></a> when this page
      is built. They are what the classifier actually uses. If you disagree with an entry, that is a legitimate
      disagreement about method, and the
      <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues`}>issue tracker</a> is the right place for it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="compliance">6. Instruction compliance</h2>

    <p>
      Reported separately from classification throughout, because they measure different
      capabilities. A model can be decisive and disobedient, or obedient and evasive. Collapsing
      them into a single score would hide the more interesting half of the result.
    </p>

    <h2 id="scores">7. Constructed scores</h2>

    <p>
      The quadrant axes and the leaderboard ranking are <strong
        >constructed measures, not observations</strong
      >. They are arithmetic applied to opinions about what matters. They are stated in full here so
      that a reader can decide how much weight to give them:
    </p>

    <dl class="scores">
      {
        SCORE_DEFINITIONS.map((definition) => (
          <div>
            <dt>
              {definition.name} <span class="range">({definition.range})</span>
            </dt>
            <dd>
              <p class="formula">
                <code>{definition.formula}</code>
              </p>
              <p>{definition.note}</p>
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <p>
      Quadrant regions are named <q>Category Definers</q>, <q>Rapid Responders</q>,
      <q>Considered Voices</q> and <q>Emerging Participants</q>. The boundaries are the medians of
      the current edition, not fixed thresholds, so a vendor's region can change because its
      competitors changed.
    </p>

    <h2 id="tokens">8. Token counts are not comparable across vendors</h2>

    <p>
      Three independent reasons, each sufficient on its own: every vendor tokenises differently;
      vendors disagree about whether reasoning tokens are counted inside output tokens; and vendors
      report prompt-cache hits differently. A live measurement in this repository found one vendor
      reporting a total of 1,295 tokens for a call whose prompt and completion summed to 648 — the
      difference being reasoning tokens counted separately.
    </p>

    <p>
      The full per-provider mapping is in
      <a href={sourceUrl('docs/usage-normalization.md')}>usage-normalization.md</a>. Do not rank
      models by token count.
    </p>

    <h2 id="cost">9. Cost estimates</h2>

    <p>
      Cost figures are <strong>estimates</strong> computed from a per-token price table recorded in <code
        >models.json</code
      >, last read {asOf}. They are not figures from an invoice. Cached input is charged at the full
      input rate, which makes every estimate a consistent upper bound rather than a
      differently-wrong number per vendor.
    </p>

    <h2 id="availability">10. Provider availability</h2>

    <p>
      Where a provider returns no usable data, that is recorded and published rather than
      suppressed. Excluding unavailable providers would bias the longitudinal record toward
      whichever vendors happened to be operating.
    </p>

    <h2 id="limitations">Limitations of this research</h2>

    <p>
      The questions are silly. Whether a hot dog is a sandwich is not a matter of fact, there is no
      correct answer, and consequently <strong
        >nothing in this publication measures model quality</strong
      >. A model that answers <q>No</q> is not better or worse than one that answers <q>Yes</q>, and
      the scores here are deliberately verdict-agnostic to avoid implying otherwise.
    </p>

    <p>
      This project exists to demonstrate how a cross-provider benchmark is built: the adapter layer,
      usage normalisation, latency measurement, sampling, versioned data, scheduled collection, and
      honest presentation. Those parts are sincere. The subject matter is a vehicle for them.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you want to use it for something real, fork it and change
      <code>questions.json</code>. The <a href={routes.howItWorks()}>how it works</a> page explains the
      pieces.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <ol class="footnotes">
      <li id="fn1">
        A compliance rate against an instruction that was never given would measure nothing.
        <a href="#fnref1" aria-label="Back to reference 1">↩</a>
      </li>
      <li id="fn2">
        Inference speed would require controlling for network position, hardware, batching and load.
        This publication controls for none of them.
        <a href="#fnref2" aria-label="Back to reference 2">↩</a>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .word-lists {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(12rem, 100%), 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-m);
    margin-block: var(--space-m);
  }

  .word-lists h3 {
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .word-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3xs);
  }

  .word-list code {
    background: var(--surface-sunken);
    padding: 0.1em 0.4em;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  .list-source {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .scores > div {
    padding: var(--space-s) 0;
    border-top: var(--hairline) solid var(--rule);
  }

  .scores dt {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text-strong);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
  }

  .scores .range {
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .scores dd {
    margin: var(--space-2xs) 0 0;
  }

  .formula code {
    background: var(--surface-sunken);
    padding: 0.2em 0.4em;
    display: inline-block;
  }

  .footnotes {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .footnotes li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2xs);
  }
</style>
